Code Quality

Keep any refactor or code additions simple, readable, and uncomplicated.
Keep code inline with the current style of existing code.
Keep things concise.
Combine methods where it makes sense and will not break existing code.
Prefer editing existing functions over creating new ones with similar purpose.
Match existing naming conventions exactly — don't introduce a new casing style or naming pattern.
Don't add comments explaining obvious code; comment only non-obvious logic or decisions.
Avoid premature abstraction — don't generalize code for hypothetical future use cases.

After any refactor or new code: delete everything that isn't 
required. No speculative abstractions, no convenience wrappers 
without multiple callers, no duplicated logic, no extra parameters. 
The goal is the minimum correct implementation. Defend every line.

If a fix reintroduces code or logic that was recently removed or
changed, stop and flag it instead of proceeding. Do not silently
cycle back to a previous state. Circular changes are a signal to
ask for clarification, not to keep iterating.

Never modify code that is not directly related to the current task.
If existing code works and is not causing the problem being solved,
leave it alone. Refactoring working code requires explicit instruction.

Testing & Validation

When modifying logic, note what should be manually tested afterward.
Don't write tests unless explicitly asked, but flag when a change seems risky enough to warrant one.


